The real trait has an eighth method (crates/fleet-daemon/src/machines/link.rs:83 `fn nudge_reconnect(&self) {}`), which `crates/fleet-daemon/src/services/bootstrap.rs:279` calls to make the post-restart probe observe the new daemon instead of waiting out a grown backoff. `grep -rn -i nudge docs/` returns nothing.

crates/fleet-daemon/src/machines/link.rs:960-971:
```rust
#[tokio::test]
async fn nudge_on_a_connected_link_does_not_disturb_it() {
    let provider = Arc::new(UnreachableProvider::new());
    let link = RemoteLink::new(Arc::clone(&provider) as Arc<dyn MachineProvider>, LinkOptions::default());
    // Not connected yet: the nudge must be inert rather than panic or spawn work.
    link.nudge_reconnect();
    assert_eq!(provider.attempts(), 0);
    assert_eq!(link.state(), LinkState::Down);
}
```
`connect()` is never called, so `started` is false and no actor exists — the assertions hold for any implementation of `nudge_reconnect`, including one that is wrong for a connected link. The trait doc says (link.rs:82) "Implementations must treat this as a no-op for a link that is already connected", but the implementation stores a `Notify` permit unconditionally (link.rs:313 `self.reconnect.notify_one();`), which `run_link` consumes at the *next* disconnect (link.rs:383 `() = reconnect.notified() => nudged = true`) and uses to reset the backoff — deliberately, per the comment at link.rs:311-312.

